    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>What is a solar CCTV camera with a SIM card?</h2>
<p>A 4G solar camera combines three things in one weatherproof unit: a battery camera, a solar panel to recharge it, and a cellular modem with a SIM card slot. Instead of joining your home Wi-Fi, it connects to the nearest cell tower, exactly like a phone. When motion wakes the camera, it records a clip, sends a push notification and uploads the video over LTE. You can open live view from anywhere with a phone signal.</p>
<p>That makes a cellular solar camera, often sold as a solar security camera with SIM card, the go-to option for places with no internet and no power: farm gates, barns, fields, construction sites, vacant lots, cabins, boat docks and long rural driveways. It also suits properties where Wi-Fi exists but doesn't reach, or where you don't want a camera to depend on a home router that goes down in a power cut.</p>
<div class="keyfacts"><div><b>1–10+ GB</b><span>typical monthly data use</span></div><div><b>5–10 W</b><span>panel size to plan for</span></div><div><b>3–5 h</b><span>direct sun a 4G camera usually needs</span></div></div>

<h2>How 4G solar cameras work</h2>
<p>Like other battery solar cameras, a 4G model spends most of its time asleep and wakes when a PIR or radar sensor detects motion. The difference is the radio. A Wi-Fi radio can sleep deeply and reconnect to a nearby router in a fraction of a second. A cellular modem has to stay registered with the network so it can receive incoming requests like live view, which means it wakes periodically even when nothing is happening. When transmitting, it can draw a watt or more in bursts, and more still when the signal is weak and the modem raises its transmit power to reach a distant tower.</p>
<p>The result is that a solar camera with a SIM card typically uses more energy per day than an equivalent Wi-Fi camera, and much more on a site with marginal coverage. The camera handles this with a larger battery, a larger panel, or both. Our guide to <a href="/how-solar-cameras-work/">how solar cameras work</a> covers the energy chain in detail.</p>
<p>Most cameras also record to a microSD card, so footage is saved locally even if the upload fails. Many offer optional cloud storage on top.</p>

<h2>Example 4G solar cameras</h2>
<p>We are a research-based publication and have not hands-on tested these; the picks below are based on manufacturer specs and owner reports. Specs and bundles change, so confirm current details, especially supported LTE bands and carriers, on the listing before you buy.</p>
<div class="product"><span class="badge">Best dedicated 4G solar camera</span><h3>Reolink 4G LTE solar camera</h3>
<ul class="spec-chips"><li>4G LTE cellular</li><li>Works without Wi-Fi</li><li>Solar powered</li><li>Needs SIM / data plan</li><li>microSD local storage</li></ul>
<p>A purpose-built cellular camera for sites with no Wi-Fi at all. It runs from a rechargeable battery topped up by solar, connects over LTE with your SIM, and records locally to microSD as a backup when signal drops. It's a good fit for gates, fields, barns and construction sites. Check the listing for the exact model's LTE bands and which carriers it supports in your country.</p>
<div class="proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Designed around cellular use</li><li>No Wi-Fi, internet or power needed on site</li><li>Local microSD backup</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Needs an ongoing data plan</li><li>Band and carrier compatibility must be checked</li><li>Higher power draw than Wi-Fi models</li></ul></div></div>
