<h2>Carrier and band compatibility: will an LTE solar security camera work on my network?</h2>
<p>This is the step most buyers skip and the most common reason a cellular camera "doesn't work." Three things must line up: the camera's LTE bands, your carrier's bands in your area, and the carrier's willingness to activate the device.</p>
<h3>Check the LTE bands</h3>
<p>Cellular networks run on numbered frequency bands. A camera only works if its modem supports at least one band your carrier uses where the camera will be mounted, ideally including the low-frequency bands that travel furthest in rural areas. In the US, the major carriers commonly use bands such as 2, 4, 12, 13, 66 and 71 for LTE, with the low bands (12, 13, 71) doing the long-distance work. In Europe and the UK, bands 1, 3, 7, 8 and 20 are common. Camera models are often sold in regional versions with different band sets, so check the exact model number. The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LTE_frequency_bands" rel="noopener" target="_blank">LTE frequency band list</a> is a handy reference.</p>
<h3>Understand the modem category</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>LTE category</th><th>Typical max speed (down / up)</th><th>Suitability for cameras</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Cat-M1 (LTE-M)</td><td>About 1 Mbps</td><td>Fine for sensors; too slow for most video</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cat-1</td><td>About 10 / 5 Mbps</td><td>Common in 4G cameras; enough for 2K clips and live view</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cat-4</td><td>About 150 / 50 Mbps</td><td>More headroom for higher resolution and faster uploads</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Real-world speeds are much lower than these maximums, especially at the edge of coverage. Upload speed matters most for a camera, since it is sending video, not receiving it.</p>
<h3>Check carrier activation</h3>
<p>Some carriers only activate devices on their approved list, and some phone plans prohibit use in other devices. Run the camera's IMEI through your carrier's compatibility checker if one is available, or ask the carrier directly. 3G networks have been shut down in many countries, so avoid any camera that relies on 3G fallback.</p>

<h2>Which SIM card and data plan should a solar security camera use?</h2>
<p>You have more options than a standard phone plan, and the right one depends on how much data the camera uses and how long it will run.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>SIM type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Good for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Data-only SIM from a major carrier</td><td>Tablet/hotspot-style plan with a monthly data allowance</td><td>Most home and farm cameras</td><td>Speed throttling after the cap; device restrictions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prepaid data SIM</td><td>Pay upfront for data; no contract</td><td>Seasonal sites, trying a camera out</td><td>Plans that expire if not topped up, taking the camera offline</td></tr>
<tr><td>IoT / M2M SIM</td><td>Business-focused plans for connected devices, often pooled data</td><td>Multiple cameras, construction sites</td><td>Minimum terms; check video use is permitted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bundled camera plan</td><td>SIM sold or promoted by the camera maker or a partner carrier</td><td>Simplest setup</td><td>Compare price per GB with alternatives</td></tr>
<tr><td>Add-on line on a family plan</td><td>Extra data line on an existing account</td><td>Low-cost single camera</td><td>Terms may not allow non-phone devices</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>At time of writing, typical US data plans suitable for a camera run about $5–$30 per month depending on allowance. Set a usage alert in your carrier account so a surge in events or a forgotten live view session doesn't surprise you.</p>
<h3>APN setup</h3>
<p>The APN (access point name) tells the camera how to connect to the carrier's data network. Many cameras detect it automatically. If the camera shows a signal but won't connect to the cloud, enter the APN manually in the app, using the value from your carrier's support site. Some IoT SIMs use a custom APN plus a username and password.</p>
<h3>SIM PIN and SIM security</h3>
<p>Many cameras can't enter a SIM PIN, so you may need to disable the PIN in a phone before inserting the SIM. The trade-off is that a stolen camera's SIM could then be used elsewhere. Reduce the risk by using a data-only plan with a usage cap, enabling account alerts, and suspending the line promptly if a camera goes missing. If the camera supports entering a PIN in its app, keep the PIN enabled.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Activate in a phone first</strong><p>Put a new SIM in a phone, confirm it has data, disable the PIN if needed, and only then move it to the camera. It removes most first-time connection problems.</p></div>

<h2>Checking 4G signal at the site</h2>
<p>Coverage maps are a starting point, not a guarantee. Before you buy, and certainly before you mount:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Check the carrier and national maps</strong><p>Look up your address on each carrier's coverage map and on the <a href="https://broadbandmap.fcc.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">FCC National Broadband Map</a>, which includes mobile coverage reported by US carriers.</p></li>
<li><strong>Test with a phone on the same carrier</strong><p>Stand at the exact mounting spot and run a speed test. Pay attention to upload speed; 1–2 Mbps or more is a comfortable target for 2K video.</p></li>
<li><strong>Read the signal numbers</strong><p>Phone field-test modes and many camera apps show RSRP. As a rough guide, better than about -90 dBm is good, -90 to -105 dBm is usable, and worse than about -110 dBm is likely to cause slow uploads and battery drain.</p></li>
<li><strong>Try different heights and sides</strong><p>Signal often improves a few feet higher or on the side of a building facing the tower. Metal roofs, barns and containers block signal; mount on the outside.</p></li>
<li><strong>Confirm in the camera app after pairing</strong><p>The camera's antenna differs from your phone's, so check its own signal reading at the mount before drilling.</p></li>
</ol>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/sim-card-solar-camera-2.webp" alt="Cellular solar camera mounted on a post with a clear line toward distant cell towers" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Mount high and on the side facing the tower for the best LTE signal.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How much data does a 4G solar camera use?</h2>
<p>Data use depends on three things: how many events the camera records, how long and how sharp each clip is, and how much live view you watch. Clips are usually the steady base load; live view is the wildcard. As a rough guide, a 20-second 2K clip at a typical camera bitrate of 1–2 Mbps is about 2.5–5 MB, and live view at around 1.5 Mbps uses roughly 11 MB per minute.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Usage pattern</th><th>Assumptions</th><th>Approx. monthly data</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Quiet gate or field</td><td>10 clips/day, 2 min live view/day</td><td>About 1.5–2.5 GB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical farm or rural driveway</td><td>30 clips/day, 5 min live view/day</td><td>About 4–6 GB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Busy site or construction yard</td><td>80 clips/day, 10 min live view/day</td><td>About 10–15 GB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alerts only, clips saved to SD</td><td>Thumbnails/notifications, occasional playback</td><td>Under 1 GB</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>These figures are estimates for planning; actual use varies with bitrate settings, scene complexity and resolution. Use the calculator below to model your own camera, events per day, clip length and live view habits, and to see how many days of footage fit on your microSD card.</p>
<!--tool:data-->
<p>You can also open the standalone <a href="/tools/4g-data-and-storage-calculator/">4G data and storage calculator</a>. To cut data use without losing coverage: lower the resolution for cloud uploads if the camera allows it, shorten clips, tighten detection zones, use person/vehicle detection, and save full-quality footage to microSD while uploading only alerts.</p>

<h2>Power draw: why a 4G solar camera needs a bigger panel</h2>
<p>Because the modem stays registered and transmits at higher power than Wi-Fi, a cellular solar camera typically uses more energy per day, often roughly 3–8 Wh depending on events, signal quality and live view. A Wi-Fi camera in a similar spot might use 1–3 Wh. Weak signal is the biggest multiplier: the modem spends longer transmitting each clip and at higher power.</p>
<p>In practice that means:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give the panel the best sun on site.</strong> A 5–10 W panel with 3–5 hours of direct sun is a sensible target.</li>
<li><strong>Angle it for winter</strong> if you need year-round reliability: roughly latitude + 15°, facing true south in the northern hemisphere. Use the <a href="/tools/solar-panel-angle-calculator/">solar panel angle calculator</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Fix the signal before upsizing the panel.</strong> An external antenna or a better mounting spot can save more energy than a bigger panel adds.</li>
<li><strong>Check the budget</strong> with the <a href="/tools/solar-camera-battery-calculator/">solar camera battery calculator</a>, entering a higher daily Wh figure for 4G.</li>
</ul>
<p>Winter is the hard season for cellular cameras: fewer sun hours plus higher draw. See <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-in-winter/">do solar cameras work in winter</a> and <a href="/guides/solar-camera-battery-life-no-sun/">solar camera battery life with no sun</a>.</p>

<h2>External antennas for weak signal</h2>
<p>Some 4G cameras have detachable antennas on standard connectors (often SMA). That lets you fit a higher-gain antenna or move the antenna to a better spot on a short cable.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Omnidirectional antennas</strong> pick up signal from all directions. A modest gain upgrade helps where signal is fair but inconsistent.</li>
<li><strong>Directional antennas</strong> (panel or Yagi style) focus on one direction and can pull in a distant tower. Aim them at the tower; tower locator apps and carrier maps help.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the cable short.</strong> Thin coax loses signal with length, which can cancel out the antenna's gain. Use low-loss cable and weatherproof the connector.</li>
<li><strong>Check compatibility.</strong> The antenna must cover the bands your carrier uses, and the camera must have a removable antenna port.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Solar camera with no Wi-Fi: 4G vs other options</h2>
<p>A SIM card camera isn't the only way to cover a spot without Wi-Fi. Compare the alternatives before you commit to a monthly data plan.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Range</th><th>Monthly cost</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>4G LTE solar camera</td><td>Anywhere with cell coverage</td><td>Data plan</td><td>Remote sites, no internet at all</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wi-Fi extender or mesh node</td><td>Tens of meters from the house</td><td>None</td><td>Sheds and yards near the house</td></tr>
<tr><td>Point-to-point wireless bridge</td><td>Hundreds of meters to kilometers with line of sight</td><td>None</td><td>Barns and outbuildings on the same property</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hub-based camera system</td><td>Often further than direct Wi-Fi</td><td>None or optional cloud</td><td>Multi-camera homes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local-only recording (microSD)</td><td>No connection</td><td>None</td><td>Evidence capture without live alerts</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Our guide to whether <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-wifi/">solar cameras need Wi-Fi</a> covers each of these in more depth.</p>

<h2>4G solar camera buying checklist</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Supports your carrier's LTE bands (exact model/region)</td><td>Wrong bands mean no connection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carrier will activate it (IMEI check)</td><td>Some carriers restrict devices</td></tr>
<tr><td>Signal at the mount: decent RSRP and 1–2 Mbps upload</td><td>Weak signal drains battery and delays alerts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data plan sized from your estimate, with usage alerts</td><td>Avoids overage or throttling</td></tr>
<tr><td>microSD local storage</td><td>Footage survives signal drops</td></tr>
<tr><td>Panel of 5–10 W, detachable, with 3–5 h direct sun</td><td>4G draws more power than Wi-Fi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Removable antenna port</td><td>Lets you upgrade for weak signal</td></tr>
<tr><td>IP65 or better, charging temperature range</td><td>Exposed rural mounts face harsh weather</td></tr>
<tr><td>Person/vehicle detection and activity zones</td><td>Fewer false alerts save data and battery</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anti-theft: height, security screws, cloud backup</td><td>Remote cameras are theft targets</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For site-specific setups, see <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-for-farms/">solar cameras for farms</a>, <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-for-construction-sites/">construction site cameras</a> and the <a href="/guides/remote-cabin-solar-camera/">remote cabin guide</a>. When you are ready to mount, follow our <a href="/solar-camera-installation/">solar camera installation guide</a>, which includes extra steps for 4G cameras. Chosen carefully, a solar CCTV camera with SIM card gives you live alerts from places no cable or Wi-Fi network will ever reach.</p>
